{
  "questions": [
    {
      "id": "q1",
      "text": "What does a for loop let you do?",
      "options": [
        "Repeat lines without writing them again",
        "Write a text on the screen",
        "Check a condition once",
        "Keep a value in a variable"
      ],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q2",
      "text": "Which numbers does `range(1, 5)` give?",
      "options": ["1, 2, 3, 4, 5", "1, 2, 3, 4", "0, 1, 2, 3, 4", "2, 3, 4, 5"],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q3",
      "text": "How many times do the lines under `for i in range(10):` run?",
      "options": ["9", "11", "10", "Once"],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q4",
      "text": "Where does `range(5)` start?",
      "options": ["From 1", "From 5", "From -1", "From 0"],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q5",
      "text": "What does the third number in `range(0, 11, 2)` say?",
      "options": [
        "By how much i jumps from one value to the next",
        "How many times the loop runs",
        "The last number the loop gives",
        "The number the loop starts from"
      ],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q6",
      "text": "What has to come at the end of a for line?",
      "options": [";", ":", ".", ","],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q7",
      "text": "Which lines belong to a for loop?",
      "options": [
        "Every line after it, to the end of the program",
        "Only the first line under it",
        "The indented lines under it",
        "The lines above it"
      ],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q8",
      "text": "When does the last line run?",
      "code": "total = 0\nfor i in range(1, 6):\n    total = total + i\nprint(total)",
      "options": [
        "On every iteration",
        "Before the loop starts",
        "Only when total is 0",
        "Once, after the loop ends"
      ],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q9",
      "text": "Why do we write `total = 0` before the loop?",
      "options": [
        "So the total starts from 0 and the loop keeps adding to it",
        "Because every loop needs a variable equal to 0",
        "So the loop runs 0 times",
        "So total goes back to 0 on every iteration"
      ],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q10",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "for i in range(1, 4):\n    total = 0\n    total = total + i\nprint(total)",
      "options": ["6", "3", "0", "1"],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q11",
      "text": "Which range gives the numbers from 1 to 10, both included?",
      "options": ["range(1, 10)", "range(0, 10)", "range(1, 11)", "range(10)"],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q12",
      "text": "Which range gives the even numbers from 2 to 10?",
      "options": ["range(2, 10, 2)", "range(2, 11)", "range(0, 10, 2)", "range(2, 11, 2)"],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q13",
      "text": "What is `in` in `for i in range(5):`?",
      "options": [
        "A keyword: it gives i the values of range() one by one",
        "A variable we have to make first",
        "A function, like print()",
        "The name of the loop"
      ],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q14",
      "text": "What is `range()`?",
      "options": [
        "A variable we make before the loop",
        "A function that already exists in Python",
        "A keyword, like if",
        "A comment for the loop"
      ],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q15",
      "text": "What do we call one pass through the lines of a loop?",
      "options": ["A range", "A step", "An iteration", "A condition"],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q16",
      "text": "How many numbers does `range(3, 8)` give?",
      "options": ["4", "8", "6", "5"],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q17",
      "text": "How many times does this print Hello?",
      "code": "for i in range(1, 4):\n    print(\"Hello\")",
      "options": ["3", "4", "1", "2"],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q18",
      "text": "Why write a for loop instead of a print() for every number?",
      "options": [
        "for prints faster than print()",
        "We don't repeat the code: the loop does the repetition for us",
        "A program can't have more than 100 print() lines",
        "A for loop needs no indentation"
      ],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q19",
      "text": "An if sits inside a for. How many times is the print() inside that if indented?",
      "options": ["Once", "Not at all", "Twice", "Three times"],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q20",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "for i in range(1, 11):\n    if i % 2 == 0:\n        print(i)",
      "options": ["1, 3, 5, 7, 9", "Every number from 1 to 10", "2, 4, 6, 8", "2, 4, 6, 8, 10"],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q21",
      "text": "What does `i % 2 == 0` check?",
      "options": [
        "That i is even: dividing it by 2 leaves nothing",
        "That i is 2",
        "That i is 0",
        "That i is odd"
      ],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q22",
      "text": "In the Energy Collector mission, which cells go into total when there are 6?",
      "options": ["1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6", "1, 3, 5", "2, 4, 6", "1, 3, 5, 7"],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q23",
      "text": "In the Energy Collector mission, why does the loop use `range(1, cells + 1)`?",
